{"builder":"Make one pull request on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test that makes packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js accept several files. Every line of issue 104's expect list must hold at the PR head.\n\nWhat is there now: packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js reads only process.argv[2]. It prints `<file>:<line>: <error>` for each bad row (newlines in the error become spaces) and exits 0 when clean, 1 on a bad row, 2 when no argument is given or the file can't be read. packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js exports lint(text). The tests are packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js and test/lint.test.js; they use node:test and spawnSync on the bin. The CI workflow is .github/workflows/test.yml, which runs `npm ci` and then `npm test` on Node 22.\n\nSteps:\n1. Rewrite packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js so it takes `<file> [<file> ...]` (all of process.argv.slice(2)):\n   - No arguments: print a usage line on stderr and exit 2.\n   - More than one argument equal to `-`: this is a usage error. Print a message on stderr and exit 2. Doing this before checking any file is the simplest choice.\n   - Check each file in the order named. A file named `-` is read from stdin (for example readFileSync(0, 'utf8')) and reported as `-`, so bad rows print as `-:<line>: <error>`. Every other file is read with readFileSync(file, 'utf8'). For each bad row print `<file>:<line>: <error>` on stdout, in exactly the same format as for one file today, using the file argument exactly as given.\n   - If a file can't be read, print `jsonl-lint: cannot read <file>: <message>` on stderr and go on to the next file. Do not exit early. The other files are still checked and reported.\n   - Exit code: 2 if any file couldn't be read. Otherwise 1 if any row in any file was bad. Otherwise 0.\n   - A single file must behave as it does today, so the existing tests still pass.\n2. Do not change lint(text) in packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js. Leave that file as it is.\n3. Add node:test tests in packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js (spawnSync the bin; pass stdin through the `input` option). Cover each of these:\n   - two clean files: exit 0, empty stdout\n   - one bad file among several clean ones: exit 1, and only the bad file's `<file>:<line>: <error>` lines appear, in order\n   - stdin via `-`, clean: exit 0\n   - stdin via `-`, bad: exit 1, and lines start with `-:`\n   - a missing file among good ones: exit 2, a message on stderr, and the other files still checked (a bad row in a good file is still printed)\n   - two `-` arguments: exit 2\n   - no arguments: exit 2\n4. Update packages/jsonl-lint/README.md. Document the usage `jsonl-lint <file> [<file> ...]`, that `-` means stdin and is reported as `-`, that only one `-` is allowed, and the exit codes 0/1/2 as listed above, including that unreadable files are reported on stderr while the other files are still checked.\n5. Run `npm test` at the repo root and make sure it passes. Add no runtime dependencies and no build step.\n6. Open one PR. Its body must contain the line `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`. Make sure the `test` workflow run on the PR head is green; if it fails, fix it and push again.","verifier":"Check out the pull request's head and run every check from the repository root. Make a scratch directory, for example D=$(mktemp -d), and in it create: a.jsonl containing `{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}\\n` (clean), b.jsonl containing `{\"c\":3}\\n` (clean), and bad.jsonl containing `{\"x\":1}\\n{bad}\\n` (bad at line 2). Let BIN=packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...] checks every file named in order and prints <file>:<line>: <error> for each bad row, as for one file before\".\nRun `node $BIN $D/a.jsonl $D/bad.jsonl $D/b.jsonl`, then `node $BIN $D/bad.jsonl $D/a.jsonl $D/bad.jsonl`, then `node $BIN $D/bad.jsonl` on its own.\nPASS when all of these hold:\n- The first run prints exactly one stdout line, which starts with `$D/bad.jsonl:2: `.\n- The second run prints `$D/bad.jsonl:2: ...` twice (once for each time the file is named, in argument order), and the error text matches the single-file run's line.\n- The single-file run prints the line `<path>:2: <JSON.parse message>`.\nFAIL if any file is skipped, the order is wrong, or the format differs from `<file>:<line>: <error>`.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"A file named - is read from stdin and reported as -; a second - is a usage error (exit 2)\".\nRun `printf '{\"a\":1}\\n{bad}\\n' | node $BIN -`, `printf '{\"a\":1}\\n' | node $BIN $D/a.jsonl -`, and `printf '{\"a\":1}\\n' | node $BIN - -` (and also `node $BIN - $D/a.jsonl -`), printing $? after each.\nPASS when all of these hold:\n- The first prints `-:2: <error>` and exits 1.\n- The second prints nothing and exits 0.\n- Both runs with two `-` arguments exit 2.\nFAIL otherwise.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Exit 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, 2 if no file is named or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported)\".\nRun these, printing $? after each:\n- `node $BIN $D/a.jsonl $D/b.jsonl` (expect 0)\n- `node $BIN $D/a.jsonl $D/bad.jsonl` (expect 1)\n- `node $BIN` with no arguments (expect 2, with a message on stderr)\n- `node $BIN $D/a.jsonl $D/missing.jsonl $D/bad.jsonl` (expect 2: stderr names the missing file, and stdout still contains `$D/bad.jsonl:2: ...`)\n- `node $BIN $D/missing.jsonl $D/a.jsonl` (expect 2, with stderr naming the missing file)\nPASS when every exit code and output is as stated. FAIL if any exit code differs, the missing-file error is missing from stderr or appears on stdout, or the files after the missing one are not checked and reported.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"lint(text) in src/index.js is unchanged\".\nRun `git diff <merge-base with main>..HEAD -- packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js`, where the merge base is `git merge-base origin/main HEAD`.\nPASS when the diff is empty. FAIL if the lint function's code changed in any way.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"node:test tests cover two clean files, one bad among several, stdin via - (clean and bad), a missing file among good ones, two - arguments and no arguments; the package README documents the usage\".\nRun `npm test` in packages/jsonl-lint, and read the test files under packages/jsonl-lint/test/ at the head.\nPASS when all of these hold:\n- The tests pass.\n- There are node:test tests that run the CLI for each of these cases: two clean files; one bad file among several; stdin via `-` that is clean; stdin via `-` that is bad; a missing file among good ones; two `-` arguments; no arguments.\n- packages/jsonl-lint/README.md documents the multi-file usage (`<file> [<file> ...]`) and `-` for stdin.\nFAIL if any listed case has no test, the tests fail, or the README does not document the usage.\n\nCheck 6. Expect line: \"The change is one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test whose body says Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104, and npm test and the test workflow are green at its head\".\nRun `gh pr view <pr> --repo suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test --json body,headRefOid`, then `gh pr checks <pr> --repo suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test` (or the check runs for the head SHA). Also run `npm ci && npm test` at the repo root.\nPASS when all of these hold:\n- The body contains `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`.\n- The `test` workflow check for the head SHA has succeeded.\n- The local `npm test` exits 0.\nFAIL if the body line is missing, the test workflow failed or did not run at the head, or npm test fails.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail):\n- Note whether the two-`-` usage error is raised before any file is read.\n- Note whether the README also lists the exit codes for several files."}